    <h1 class="font-serif-sc text-3xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-bold leading-tight tracking-tight text-stone-900 mt-3">Sample Itineraries for Your Traditional Wear Trip</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-stone-500 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">Four routes built around Chinese traditional clothing experiences, each combining hands-on costume activities with essential sightseeing. Pick the one that fits your timeline and interests — or mix and match segments.</p>

    <!-- Itinerary 1: 5-day Xi'an -->
    <div class="mt-10 bg-stone-50 rounded-xl p-6">
      <span class="text-xs px-2.5 py-1 bg-stone-500 text-white rounded-full font-medium">5 Days</span>
      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-3">Hanfu Deep Dive: Xi'an Immersion</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 mt-2 leading-relaxed">One city, maximum costume time. If you only have a few days, Xi'an rewards you with the highest concentration of Hanfu rental shops, the most photogenic ancient backdrop, and the best value.</p>
      <div class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-600">
        <p><strong>Day 1 — Arrive Xi'an:</strong> Check into hotel near South Gate (Yongningmen). Evening: Bell Tower & Drum Tower lit up at night, stroll the Muslim Quarter for street food — try roujiamo (肉夹馍, Chinese burger) and yangrou paomo (羊肉泡馍, lamb soup with flatbread).</p>
        <p><strong>Day 2 — First Hanfu Day:</strong> Morning: arrive at a Hanfu rental shop near South Gate by 9 AM (beat the crowds). Budget 1 hour for selection, fitting, hair, and makeup. Full day in costume on the city wall — rent a bike (45 RMB) and ride the full 13.7 km perimeter. Late afternoon: change dynasty style, head to Daming Palace National Heritage Park for golden hour. Evening: Datang Everbright City (大唐不夜城) — a pedestrian strip where locals and tourists alike walk in full Hanfu.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 3 — Warriors & History:</strong> Morning: half-day trip to Terracotta Warriors (book through hotel or Didi, ~1 hr east of Xi'an). Afternoon: Shaanxi History Museum (free, but book a reservation 3+ days ahead on WeChat). Evening: Tang Dynasty Music and Dance Show (Tang Yue Gong, 唐乐宫).</p>
        <p><strong>Day 4 — Second Hanfu Day:</strong> Try a different dynasty. Morning: Small Wild Goose Pagoda (小雁塔) & Jianfu Temple — quieter, fewer tourists, excellent photo backdrop. Afternoon: Qujiang Pool Park (曲江池遗址公园) for waterside Hanfu shots. Evening: one last walk through Datang Everbright City.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 5 — Departure:</strong> Morning shopping around the Muslim Quarter for silk scarves, shadow puppets, and souvenirs. Afternoon departure from Xi'an Xianyang Airport (XIY) or Xi'an North Railway Station.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-stone-400 mt-4">Best months: April (Hanfu Culture Festival), May, October. | Budget: 3,200–5,500 RMB per person (excluding flights). | Where to stay: Bell Tower area or near South Gate metro.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Itinerary 2: 7-day Shanghai-Suzhou-Hangzhou -->
    <div class="mt-8 bg-stone-50 rounded-xl p-6">
      <span class="text-xs px-2.5 py-1 bg-[#b5343a] text-white rounded-full font-medium">7 Days</span>
      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-3">Qipao & Silk Trail: Shanghai → Suzhou → Hangzhou</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 mt-2 leading-relaxed">For travelers who prefer elegance over drama — custom qipao tailoring, silk markets, classical gardens, and tea culture across three of China's most sophisticated cities.</p>
      <div class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-600">
        <p><strong>Day 1 — Shanghai, First Fitting:</strong> Arrive Shanghai. Head to South Bund Fabric Market (南外滩轻纺面料市场) at 399 Lujiabang Road — three floors of tailors. Get measured for a custom qipao (bring reference photos). Choose fabric and style; pay 30-50% deposit. Evening: Bund riverfront walk.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 2 — Shanghai, Vintage & Glamour:</strong> Morning: French Concession walking tour — Fuxing Road and Wukang Road plane-tree lanes. Visit a vintage qipao shop like Shiatzy Chen or Han Zu for inspiration. Afternoon: Shanghai History Museum for original 1930s qipaos. Evening: cocktail at a Bund rooftop bar — the perfect setting to debut your qipao if the fitting is ready.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 3 — Suzhou Day Trip (25 min by train):</strong> Morning train to Suzhou. Rent a silk qipao near Pingjiang Road (平江路) — shops here specialize in Suzhou-style qipao with delicate embroidery. Photoshoot in the Humble Administrator's Garden (拙政园, 80 RMB entry) — arrive at opening (7:30 AM) for empty pavilions. Afternoon: shop for hand-painted silk fans and embroidered pouches along Pingjiang Road and Shantang Street. Return to Shanghai evening.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 4 — Hangzhou, Silk & Song:</strong> Morning train to Hangzhou (45 min). First stop: China National Silk Museum (中国丝绸博物馆) — free entry, world's largest textile museum, with costume try-on sessions. Rent Song dynasty-style Hanfu near the museum. Afternoon: West Lake walk in costume — start at Broken Bridge (断桥), walk the Su Causeway (苏堤).</p>
        <p><strong>Day 5 — Hangzhou, Tea & Tailoring:</strong> Morning: Longjing Village (龙井村) for tea tasting at a plantation. Rent a lighter summer Hanfu for tea field photos. Afternoon: Hangzhou Silk City — multi-story market for silk scarves, brocade, and ready-made qipao. Better prices than Shanghai. Evening: Hefang Street (河坊街) for street food and souvenir shopping.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 6 — Return to Shanghai, Final Fitting:</strong> Morning train back to Shanghai (45 min). Pick up custom qipao from South Bund — most tailors do adjustments on the spot. Afternoon: Shanghai Museum (free) or Tianzifang arts district. Evening: farewell dinner in a French Concession villa restaurant.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 7 — Departure:</strong> Morning free for last shopping. Depart from Shanghai Pudong (PVG) or Hongqiao (SHA).</p>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-stone-400 mt-4">Best months: March–May, October–November. | Budget: 5,000–9,000 RMB per person. | Custom qipao turnaround: 3–5 days (start Day 1).</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Itinerary 3: 10-day Ethnic Textile Trail (NEW, unique to this page) -->
    <div class="mt-8 bg-stone-50 rounded-xl p-6">
      <span class="text-xs px-2.5 py-1 bg-[#b5343a] text-white rounded-full font-medium">10 Days</span>
      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-3">Ethnic Textile Trail: Guizhou & Yunnan</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 mt-2 leading-relaxed">For textile lovers and off-the-beaten-path travelers. This route dives into the living textile traditions of China's ethnic minorities — Miao silver and embroidery, Bai tie-dye, and Yi wool crafts — in the mountain villages where these techniques have been practiced for a thousand years.</p>
      <div class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-600">
        <p><strong>Day 1 — Arrive Guiyang:</strong> Fly into Guiyang Longdongbao Airport (KWE). Settle into hotel. Evening: explore Guiyang's night markets — try siwawa (丝娃娃, spring roll wraps) and changwang mian (肠旺面, intestine and blood noodle soup). Guiyang is the most underrated food city in China.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 2 — Guiyang to Kaili (40 min by train):</strong> Morning high-speed train to Kaili (凯里), the gateway to Miao country. Check into hotel. Afternoon: Kaili Ethnic Minorities Museum for context on Miao textile traditions. Evening: Kaili night market.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 3 — Xijiang Miao Village (西江千户苗寨):</strong> Full day at China's largest Miao village — over 1,000 households cascading down a mountainside. Rent full Miao silver headdress and embroidered festival costume (50–100 RMB from village shops). Walk the village in costume, visit a silversmith's workshop to see how the elaborate headdresses are made. Evening: the village lights up tier by tier — one of China's most spectacular views.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 4 — Miao Embroidery Villages:</strong> Morning: visit Shidong (施洞) or Taijiang (台江) villages near Kaili — these are working textile communities, not tourist sites. Watch Miao women doing cross-stitch and counted-thread embroidery, techniques passed down for over 1,000 years. Buy embroidered bags, belts, and fabric pieces directly from artisans (50–200 RMB). Afternoon: visit a Miao batik workshop — learn wax-resist dyeing on cotton, create your own piece to take home.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 5 — Kaili Textile Market + Travel to Kunming:</strong> Morning: Kaili Sunday textile market (if timing aligns) or the daily market for last-minute embroidery purchases. Afternoon: train back to Guiyang, then flight or overnight train to Kunming (昆明).</p>
        <p><strong>Day 6 — Kunming, Yunnan Ethnic Village:</strong> Morning: Yunnan Nationalities Village (云南民族村) — a park showcasing 25 ethnic minorities with live textile and craft demonstrations. Rent Bai, Yi, or Dai costumes on site (30–80 RMB). Afternoon: Yunnan Provincial Museum for ancient Dian kingdom bronze and textile artifacts. Evening: try Crossing-the-Bridge Noodles (过桥米线), Kunming's signature dish.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 7 — Dali, Bai Tie-Dye (2 hrs by train):</strong> Morning train to Dali (大理). Afternoon: Zhoucheng Village (周城), the home of Bai tie-dye (扎染). Visit a family workshop — the Yan family has run theirs for generations — and make your own indigo tie-dyed scarf or wall hanging (50–120 RMB including materials). Wear a Bai minority costume (white top, embroidered apron, floral headdress) for photos against the indigo-draped courtyard.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 8 — Dali Old Town & Erhai Lake:</strong> Morning: Dali Ancient City — rent a Bai costume and walk the cobblestone streets. Visit a local market for embroidered Bai shoes and silver jewelry. Afternoon: cycle or e-bike around Erhai Lake (洱海) — stop at fishing villages and waterside temples. Evening: Foreigner Street (洋人街) for Yunnan coffee and craft beer.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 9 — Dali to Kunming, Last Shopping:</strong> Morning: last visit to Zhoucheng for any unfinished tie-dye pieces. Train back to Kunming. Afternoon: Kunming Bird and Flower Market for textiles, Yunnan tea, and souvenirs.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 10 — Departure:</strong> Fly out from Kunming Changshui Airport (KMG).</p>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-stone-400 mt-4">Best months: April–May, September–October. Rainy season June–August. | Budget: 5,500–9,500 RMB per person. | Pack light, breathable fabrics — it's humid. | Bring cash: village artisans rarely accept digital payments.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Itinerary 4: 14-day Silk Road of Style (from hub but expanded) -->
    <div class="mt-8 bg-stone-50 rounded-xl p-6">
      <span class="text-xs px-2.5 py-1 bg-[#b5343a] text-white rounded-full font-medium">14 Days</span>
      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-3">Silk Road of Style: Beijing → Nanjing → Hangzhou → Suzhou → Shanghai → Xi'an</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 mt-2 leading-relaxed">The grand tour. Every major traditional clothing experience in one trip: Tang suit tailoring in Beijing hutongs, Ming dynasty Hanfu in Nanjing, Song dynasty elegance in Hangzhou, silk qipao in Suzhou, custom cheongsam in Shanghai, and the full Hanfu immersion in Xi'an.</p>
      <div class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-600">
        <p><strong>Days 1–2 — Beijing:</strong> Day 1: tang suit workshop in a Nanluoguxiang hutong tailor shop — choose fabric, get measured. Day 2: Forbidden City tour, wear your tang suit for sunset photos at Jingshan Park (景山公园) overlooking the palace. Evening: Peking duck in Qianmen.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 3 — Beijing → Nanjing (4.5 hrs by train):</strong> Travel day. Afternoon: Ming Xiaoling Mausoleum (明孝陵) — the golden-roofed pathway lined with stone animals is one of China's best Hanfu photo spots. Rent Ming dynasty Hanfu at shops near the entrance.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 4 — Nanjing:</strong> Morning: Yunjin Brocade Museum (南京云锦博物馆) — see artisans weaving silk brocade on giant wooden looms, a UNESCO Intangible Heritage craft. Afternoon: Confucius Temple (夫子庙) area — qipao shops and the Qinhuai River. Evening: Qinhuai River night cruise.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 5 — Nanjing → Hangzhou (1.5 hrs by train):</strong> Travel morning. Afternoon: China National Silk Museum. Rent Song dynasty Hanfu and walk West Lake.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 6 — Hangzhou:</strong> Morning: Longjing tea village in light Hanfu. Afternoon: Lingyin Temple (灵隐寺) — one of China's oldest and most atmospheric Buddhist temples. Hangzhou Silk City for shopping.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 7 — Hangzhou → Suzhou (1.5 hrs by train) → Shanghai (25 min):</strong> Morning: Suzhou day trip — silk qipao rental, Humble Administrator's Garden photoshoot, Pingjiang Road accessories shopping. Evening train to Shanghai.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 8 — Shanghai:</strong> Morning: South Bund Fabric Market — get measured for custom qipao. Afternoon: French Concession walking tour. Evening: Bund at night.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 9 — Shanghai → Xi'an (6 hrs by train or 2.5 hrs by flight):</strong> Travel day. Evening: Bell Tower and Drum Tower, Muslim Quarter.</p>
        <p><strong>Days 10–11 — Xi'an, Full Hanfu:</strong> Day 10: Tang dynasty Hanfu — city wall, Daming Palace, Datang Everbright City. Day 11: Terracotta Warriors in the morning, then switch to Ming dynasty Hanfu for Shaanxi History Museum area and Small Wild Goose Pagoda. Evening: farewell Hanfu dinner at a Tang-themed restaurant.</p>
        <p><strong>Day 12 — Xi'an → Beijing (4.5 hrs by train):</strong> Return to Beijing. Afternoon: pick up custom tang suit from the hutong tailor (if ready). Evening: 798 Art District for contemporary Chinese art and design shops.</p>
        <p><strong>Days 13–14 — Beijing Departure:</strong> Day 13: Great Wall at Mutianyu (less crowded than Badaling) — wear your tang suit for the cable car photos. Farewell Peking duck dinner. Day 14: last souvenir shopping at Panjiayuan Antique Market. Depart from Beijing Capital Airport (PEK).</p>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-stone-400 mt-4">Best months: April–May, September–October. Avoid National Day (Oct 1–7) and Spring Festival. | Budget: 12,000–22,000 RMB per person. | Train total: ~2,000–3,000 RMB for 6 segments. | Custom tang suit: order Day 1, pick up Day 12. | Custom qipao: order Day 8, shipped or picked up if returning through Shanghai.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-14 mb-4">How to Customize These Itineraries</h2>
    <div class="space-y-3 text-stone-600 leading-relaxed text-sm">
      <p><strong>Add 1–2 buffer days in each city.</strong> Most first-timers underestimate how much time Hanfu and qipao activities take — budget minimum 4–5 hours for a full rental + photoshoot session, not including travel to the site.</p>
      <p><strong>For photographers:</strong> Book Hanfu sessions for early morning (8–10 AM) when sites are emptiest, or late afternoon (3–5 PM) for golden hour. Avoid 11 AM–2 PM — harsh overhead light and peak crowds.</p>
      <p><strong>Combine routes.</strong> Many travelers pair the 7-day Shanghai-Suzhou-Hangzhou route with a 4-day Xi'an add-on for a complete qipao + Hanfu trip. If time is tight, drop Nanjing and add those days to Xi'an.</p>
      <p><strong>Avoid Chinese public holidays.</strong> Golden Week (Oct 1–7), Spring Festival (late Jan/Feb), and Labor Day (May 1–5) see every site packed at 3–5x normal crowds. Train tickets sell out, Hanfu rental prices jump 30–50%, and photos become nearly impossible.</p>
      <p><strong>Solo travelers:</strong> Book group Hanfu tours through Xiaohongshu (小红书) or Ctrip — they pair you with other solo travelers for shared photoshoots and split photographer costs. Many shops in Xi'an offer a "solo traveler package" that includes a photographer for the first hour.</p>
      <p><strong>For families:</strong> Hanfu shops in Xi'an and Luoyang carry children's costumes. The Xi'an city wall has tandem bikes with child seats. Dali and Guizhou are excellent for families — slower pace, pedestrian-friendly old towns, and hands-on craft workshops kids love.</p>
    </div>

